A||||2||Tom88gt||what kind of frame is it???||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||01-12-2000||09:58 AM||||Just curious.  Not important, but.....<P>What kind of frame does the Fiero have?  I've heard is refered to as a space frame but I thought those were full tube steel frames (race car type).<P>Is it a unibody with plastic outer panels?<P>Is it a full frame with a body framework and panels?<P>Is it the same as the Corvette in design?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||01-12-2000||10:55 AM||||It is called a space frame.  Basically the frame is a complete car.....meaning that you can remove every single body panel and it won't lose any of it's structural integrity.  <P>A uni-body design is what most cars are today.  Usually the rear half of the cars body (C pillar) is an integral part of the frame.   <P>A full frame car is one that the body can be separated from the chassis....the Corvette is like this, as well s most older American cars.  The Fiero definitely does not fall into this category.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||01-13-2000||12:02 AM||||Instead of using steel tubes, for a mass produced car stamped steel creates as much if not more strength as those steel tube chassis. The cost is more weight though. The MR-2 was always way lighter than Fieros but Fieros had the power to move the weight.<P>   Just before the end of production Alcan and Entech were playing around with a Fiero chassis in ALUMINUM! It had a Quad 4 (They didn't know...) and was bonded together with no welding. It weighed 68% LESS than the steel and boosted torsional stiffness 42% and bending stiffness by 54%. THEY WERE BUILT IN 1986! (source: Fiero by Gary Witzenburg - Gotta LOVE this book!) All this years before the Saturns!||||reg||4
